  "lru-cache": {
    "trick": "A hash map gives O(1) lookup and a doubly linked list gives O(1) reordering: most recent at the head, evict from the tail. Store the key in each node so eviction can delete it from the map.",
    "steps": [
      "map: key → node. The list has sentinel head and tail nodes.",
      "get: move the node to the front and return its value.",
      "put: replace or insert at the front. If over capacity, remove tail.prev and delete its key."
    ],
    "time": "O(1) per operation",
    "space": "O(capacity)",
    "pitfall": "Forgetting to store the key in the node (you can't delete the evicted entry from the map). In Python, OrderedDict with move_to_end is the short answer, but interviewers usually want the real structure.",
    "code": "class LRUCache:\n    class _Node:\n        __slots__ = (\"key\", \"val\", \"prev\", \"next\")\n\n        def __init__(self, key=0, val=0):\n            self.key, self.val, self.prev, self.next = key, val, None, None\n\n    def __init__(self, capacity: int):\n        self.cap = capacity\n        self.map = {}\n        self.head, self.tail = self._Node(), self._Node()\n        self.head.next, self.tail.prev = self.tail, self.head\n\n    def _remove(self, node):\n        node.prev.next, node.next.prev = node.next, node.prev\n\n    def _add_front(self, node):\n        node.prev, node.next = self.head, self.head.next\n        self.head.next.prev = node\n        self.head.next = node\n\n    def get(self, key: int) -> int:\n        if key not in self.map:\n            return -1\n        node = self.map[key]\n        self._remove(node)\n        self._add_front(node)\n        return node.val\n\n    def put(self, key: int, value: int) -> None:\n        if key in self.map:\n            self._remove(self.map[key])\n        node = self._Node(key, value)\n        self.map[key] = node\n        self._add_front(node)\n        if len(self.map) > self.cap:\n            lru = self.tail.prev\n            self._remove(lru)\n            del self.map[lru.key]"
  },
